==English==
{{wp}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/meɪk/|[meɪkʲ]}}
* {{IPA|en|[meːk]|a=CA,MLE}}
* {{IPA|en|/mɛk/|a=Northumbrian}}
** {{IPA|en|/mæk/|a=Wearside}}
* {{audio|en|en-uk-to make.ogg|a=UK|text=to make}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-make.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪk|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:maken|tree=1}}
{{etymid|en|make}}
From {{der|en|enm|maken}}, {{m|enm|make}}, from {{der|en|ang|macian|t=to build, make, work}}, from {{der|en|gmw-pro|*makōn|t=to build, make, work}}, from {{m|gmw-pro|*mak|t=agreeable, comfortable, fit, suitable}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*makaz|t=agreeable, comfortable, fit, suitable}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*meh₂ǵ-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*meh₂ḱ-|t=to knead}} or {{m|ine-pro|*meg-|t=to be comfortable, pleasant}}. Related to {{m|en|match}}.
{{col-top|2|cog}}
Cognate with {{cog|sco|mak|t=to make}}, {{cog|yol|maak}}, {{m|yol|maake|t=to make}}, {{cog|frr|maage}}, {{m|frr|maaki}}, {{m|frr|mååge|t=to make}}, {{cog|stq|moakje|t=to make; to repair; to prepare; to preach; to earn}}, {{cog|fy|meitsje|t=to make}}, {{cog|gsw|mache|t=to make; to do}}, {{cog|bar|måcha}}, {{m|bar|måchn|t=to make; to do; to go; to cause; to play; to matter; to look}}, {{cog|cim|machan|t=to make}}, {{cog|nl,nds|maken|t=to make}}, {{cog|de|machen|t=to make; to do; to go; to cause; to play; to fare; to look}}, {{cog|lb|maachen}}, {{m|lb|man|t=to make; to do; to take; to equal; to put}}, {{cog|mhn|mòchen|t=to make}}, {{cog|wym|mahha|t=to make; to do}}, {{cog|yi|מאַכן|t=to create, make; to do; to say}}; also probably {{cog|ga|mám|t=yoke; duty, obligation; function, service}}, {{cog|la|mācerō|t=to soften; to weaken; to mortify; to torture}}, {{cog|grc|μάσσω|t=to handle, knead; to wipe}}, {{cog|bg|ма́жа|t=to smear; to oil}}, {{cog|cs|mazat|t=to lubricate; to spread; to smear; to scribble; to erase}}, {{cog|pl|mazać|t=to smear; to scribble; to pule, whimper}}, {{cog|ru|ма́зать|t=to oil; to paint; to smear; to soil; to miss}}, {{cog|sh|ма̏зати}}, {{m|sh|mȁzati|t=to smear; to dirty}}, {{cog|uk|ма́зати|t=to smear}}, {{cog|xcl|մած|t=glued together, joined; coagulated, curdled; adhesive, sticking; compact; dense}}, {{cog|hi|मचाना|t=to cause, raise, wreak}}.
{{col-bottom}}

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|en|mak||Wearside|Durham|dialectal}}
* {{alter|en|makee||pronunciation spelling}}
* {{alter|en|myek||Tyneside|dialectal}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb|make<,,made:maked[dialectal or obsolete]>}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[create]].
## To [[build]], [[construct]], [[produce]], or [[originate]].
##: {{syn|en|fabricate|Thesaurus:build}}
##: {{ux|en|We '''made''' a bird feeder for our yard.}}
##: {{ux|en|I'll '''make''' a man out of him yet.}}
##: {{ux|en|He '''makes''' deodorants.}}
##* {{RQ:Besant Ivory Gate|chapter=Prologue|passage=Thus, when he drew up instructions in lawyer language, he expressed the important words by an initial, a medial, or a final consonant, and '''made''' scratches for all the words between; his clerks, however, understood him very well.}}
##* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=7|passage=I '''made''' a speaking trumpet of my hands and commenced to whoop “Ahoy!” and “Hello!” at the top of my lungs. {{...}} The Colonel woke up, and, after asking what in brimstone was the matter, opened his mouth and roared “Hi!” and “Hello!” like the bull of Bashan.}}
##* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-06-22|volume=407|issue=8841|page=68|url=http://www.economist.com/news/finance-and-economics/21579860-g8-pledges-tackle-three-ts-t-time|title=T time
|passage=Yet in “Through a Latte, Darkly”, a new study of how Starbucks has largely avoided paying tax in Britain, Edward Kleinbard{{...}}shows that current tax rules make it easy for all sorts of firms to generate what he calls “stateless income”:{{nb...}}. In Starbucks’s case, the firm has in effect turned the process of '''making''' an expensive cup of coffee into intellectual property.|archiveurl=https://archive.ph/JN1ea|archivedate=2023-03-11}}
## To [[write]] or [[compose]].
##: {{ux|en|I '''made''' a poem for her wedding.}}
##: {{ux|en|He '''made''' a will.}}
## To [[bring about]]; to [[effect]] or [[produce]] by means of some action.
##: {{ux|en|'''make''' war}}
##: {{ux|en|They were just a bunch of ne'er-do-wells who went around '''making''' trouble for honest men.}}
##* {{RQ:en:New York Times|author=Hannah Selinger|title=Who Is Still Buying VHS Tapes?|date=2021-02-20|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/02/20/style/vhs-tapes.html|passage=But whether or not the VCR '''makes''' a complete comeback, VHS enthusiasts agree that these tapes occupy an irreplaceable place in culture.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220328033549/https://www.nytimes.com/2021/02/20/style/vhs-tapes.html}}
## {{lb|en|religious}} To [[create]] (the universe), especially {{lb|en|in Christianity}} from [[nothing]].
##: {{ux|en|God '''made''' earth and heaven.}}
##* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|part=prologue|||Thine are these orbs of light and shade;<br>⁠Thou '''madest''' Life in man and brute;<br>⁠Thou '''madest''' Death; and lo, thy foot<br>Is on the skull which thou hast '''made'''.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[prepare]] (food); to [[cook]] (food).
##: {{ux|en|I'm '''making''' cereal for breakfast. Who wants some?}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|now mostly|_|colloquial}} To [[behave]], to [[act]].
#: {{ux|en|To '''make''' like a deer caught in the headlights.}}
#: {{ux|en|They [[make nice|'''made''' nice]] together, as if their fight never happened.}}
#: {{ux|en|He [[make as if|'''made''' as if]] to punch him, but they both laughed and shook hands.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To tend; to contribute; to have effect; with ''for'' or ''against''.
#* {{RQ:Arnold Literature and Dogma|passage=And all Israel's language about this power, except that it '''makes''' for righteousness, is approximate language}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Romans|14|19|passage=Follow after the things which '''make''' for peace.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-1|act=V|scene=i|passage=Considerations infinite <br> Do '''make''' against it.}}
# To [[constitute]].
#: {{ux|en|They '''make''' a cute couple.}}
#: {{ux|en|This '''makes''' the third infraction.}}
#: {{ux|en|[[one swallow does not a summer make|One swallow does not a summer '''make'''.]]}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=5| passage=We '''made''' an odd party before the arrival of the Ten, particularly when the Celebrity dropped in for lunch or dinner. He could not be induced to remain permanently at Mohair because Miss Trevor was at Asquith, but he appropriated a Hempstead cart from the Mohair stables and made the trip sometimes twice in a day.}}
-->'''1995''', ''Harriette Simpson Arnow: Critical Essays on Her Work'', p.46:
#* {{RQ:Guardian|date=23 September 2014|author=A teacher|title=Choosing a primary school: a teacher's guide for parents|url=http://www.theguardian.com/education/2014/sep/23/choosing-primary-school-guide-teachers-guide-for-parents
|passage=So if your prospective school is proudly displaying that "We Are Outstanding" banner on its perimeter fence, well, that is wonderful … but do bear in mind that in all likelihood it has been awarded for results in those two subjects, rather than for its delivery of a broad and balanced curriculum which brings out the best in every child. Which is, of course, what '''makes''' a great primary school.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[add up to]], have a [[sum]] of.
#: {{ux|en|Two and four '''make''' six.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|construed with ''of''|typically interrogative}} To [[interpret]].
#: {{ux|en|I don’t know what to '''make''' of it.}}
#: {{ux|en|They couldn't '''make''' anything of the inscription.}}
#: {{ux|en|What time do you '''make''' it?}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|usually stressed}} To bring into [[success]].
#: {{ux|en|This company is what '''made''' you.}}
#: {{ux|en|She married into wealth and so has it '''made'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Annus Mirabilis|passage=who '''makes''' or ruins with a smile or frown}}
# {{lb|en|ditransitive|second object is an adjective or participle}} To [[cause]] to be.
#: {{synonyms|en|render}}
#: {{ux|en|The blisters on my foot '''made''' it a problem to walk without a cane.}}
#: {{ux|en|Caesar '''made''' rich men of his supporters back in Rome.}}
#: {{ux|en|The citizens '''made''' their objections clear.}}
#: {{ux|en|This might '''make''' you a bit woozy.}}
#: {{ux|en|Did I '''make''' myself heard?}}
#: {{ux|en|Scotch will '''make''' you a man.}}
#: {{ux|en|Mom '''made''' it clear what she expected from me in college.}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-07-20|volume=408|issue=8845| url=http://www.economist.com/news/http://www.economist.com/news/business/21582001-army-new-online-courses-scaring-wits-out-traditional-universities-can-they| title=The attack of the MOOCs| passage=Since the launch early last year of&nbsp;[&hellip;] two Silicon Valley start-ups offering free education through MOOCs, massive open online courses, the ivory towers of academia have been shaken to their foundations. University brands built in some cases over centuries have been forced to contemplate the possibility that information technology will rapidly '''make''' their existing business model obsolete.}}
# To cause to appear to be; to represent as.
#: {{ux|en|Homer '''makes''' Aphrodite the daughter of Zeus, unlike Hesiod who depicted her as born from the sea foam.}}
#* '''1709–1710''', {{w|Thomas Baker (antiquarian)|Thomas Baker}}, ''Reflections on Learning''
#* {{RQ:Ferguson Zollenstein|IV
|passage=So this was my future home, I thought! Certainly it '''made''' a brave picture. I had seen similar ones fired-in on many a Heidelberg stein. Backed by towering hills,{{...}}a sky of palest Gobelin flecked with fat, fleecy little clouds, it in truth looked a dear little city; the city of one's dreams.}}
# {{lb|en|ditransitive|second object is a verb}} To [[cause]] (to do something); to [[compel]] (to do something).
#: {{ux|en|You're '''making''' her cry.}}
#: {{ux|en|I was '''made''' to feel like a criminal.}}
#* {{RQ:Besant Ivory Gate|chapter=III|passage=In former days every tavern of repute kept such a room for its own select circle, a club, or society, of habitués, who met every evening, for a pipe and a cheerful glass.{{...}}Strangers might enter the room, but they were '''made''' to feel that they were there on sufferance: they were received with distance and suspicion.}}
# {{lb|en|ditransitive|second object is a verb|can be stressed for emphasis or clarity}} To [[force]] to do.
#: {{ux|en|The teacher '''made''' the student study harder.}}
#: {{ux|en|Don’t let them '''make''' you suffer.}}
# {{lb|en|ditransitive|of a fact}} To [[indicate]] or [[suggest]] to be.
#: {{ux|en|His past mistakes don’t '''make''' him a bad person.}}
# {{senseid|en|bed}}{{lb|en|transitive|of a bed}} To cover [[neat]]ly with [[bedclothes]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|US|_|slang|crime|law enforcement}} To [[recognise]], [[identify]], [[spot]].
#: {{syn|en|twig|notice|Thesaurus:identify}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1939|author=w:Raymond Chandler|title=w:The Big Sleep|page=33|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2011
|passage=I caught sight of him two or three times and then '''made''' him turning north into Laurel Canyon Drive.}}
#* '''2004''', George Nolfi et al., ''{{w|Ocean's Twelve}}'', Warner Bros. Pictures, 0:50:30:
#* '''2007''' May 4, Andrew Dettmann et al., "Under Pressure", episode 3-22 of ''{{w|Numb3rs}}'', 00:01:16:
#*: <span style="font-variant:small-caps">David Sinclair:</span> {{qualifier|walking}} Almost at Seventh; I should have a visual any second now. {{qualifier|rounds a corner, almost collides into Kaleed Asan}} Damn, that was close.<br /><span style="font-variant:small-caps">Don Eppes:</span> David, he '''make''' you?<br /><span style="font-variant:small-caps">David Sinclair:</span> No, I don't think so.
# {{lb|en|transitive|colloquial}} To [[arrive]] at a [[destination]], usually at or by a certain time.
#: {{ux|en|We should '''make''' Cincinnati by 7 tonight.}}
#: {{ux|en|I won't be able to '''make''' dinner; something's come up at work.}}
#* {{RQ:Browne Pseudodoxia Epidemica|edition=2nd|passage=They that sail in the middle can '''make''' no land of either side.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|colloquial}} To proceed (in a direction).
#: {{ux|en|They '''made''' westward over the snowy mountains.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Make''' for the hills! It's a wildfire!}}
#: {{ux|en|They '''made''' away from the fire toward the river.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1942|month=July-August|author=Philip Spencer|title=On the Footplate in Egypt|journal=Railway Magazine|page=208|text=As the guard's whistle shrilled the "right away," I '''made''' to join my companions in the train, but with a smile the driver, whose name was Abdul, bade me take the fireman's seat.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cover (a given distance) by [[travelling]]. {{defdate|from 16<sup>th</sup>c.}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=2|passage=I had occasion […] to '''make''' a somewhat long business trip to Chicago, and on my return […] I found Farrar awaiting me in the railway station. He smiled his wonted fraction by way of greeting, […], and finally leading me to his buggy, turned and drove out of town. I was completely mystified at such an unusual proceeding.}}
#* {{RQ:Burroughs Land That Time Forgot|chapter=VIII|passage=I '''made''' over twenty miles that day, for I was now hardened to fatigue and accustomed to long hikes, having spent considerable time hunting and exploring in the immediate vicinity of camp.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[move]] at (a speed). {{defdate|from 17<sup>th</sup>c.}}
#: {{ux|en|The ship could '''make''' 20 knots an hour in calm seas.}}
#: {{ux|en|This baby can '''make''' 220 miles an hour.}}
# To [[appoint]]; to [[name]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1991|author=Bernard Guenée|title=Between Church and State: The Lives of Four French Prelates|isbn=0226310329
|passage=On November 15, 1396,{{...}}Benedict XIII '''made''' him bishop of Noyon;}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|slang}} To [[induct]] into the [[Mafia]] or a similar organization (as a [[made man]]).
#* {{quote-av|en|year=1990|author=Nicholas Pileggi; Martin Scorsese|title=w:Goodfellas
|passage=<span style="font-variant:small-caps">Jimmy Conway:</span> They're gonna '''make''' him.<br><span style="font-variant:small-caps">Henry Hill:</span> Paulie's gonna '''make''' you?}}
